1
00:00:00,960 --> 00:00:08,843
Welcome to Iran's provinces, part 2 of 2. First, I clear the chosen names for
a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09,504 --> 00:00:21,358
We work through 15 of the Iran regions the map names, once each: where it
sits, one fact worth keeping, and a hook to remember it by.

3
00:00:22,042 --> 00:00:25,316
Let's bring Razavi Khorasan into view.

4
00:00:31,430 --> 00:00:41,240
Razavi Khorasan fills the north-east of Iran around Mashhad, its largest city,
on the road east towards Afghanistan and Turkmenistan.

5
00:00:41,904 --> 00:00:53,456
The name Razavi is for Imam Reza, the eighth imam of Shia Islam, whose shrine
is at Mashhad, and most of the world’s saffron is grown in this province.

6
00:00:54,113 --> 00:01:02,124
Remember the eastern province with the great shrine at Mashhad, the tomb of
the poet Ferdowsi at Tus and the saffron fields around it.

7
00:01:02,813 --> 00:01:05,065
Let's bring Kermanshah into view.

8
00:01:10,148 --> 00:01:21,410
Kermanshah lies in the west of Iran at the foot of the Zagros, with the city
of Kermanshah as its largest city, south of Kurdistan and north of Ilam.

9
00:01:22,069 --> 00:01:38,233
The province was called Bakhtaran after the revolution until its historical
name was restored in the early 1990s, and at Bisotun Darius recorded his
victories in three languages, which helped scholars read cuneiform.

10
00:01:38,885 --> 00:01:47,627
Remember the province whose name was changed and changed back, with the cliff
inscription that unlocked the cuneiform script.

11
00:01:48,313 --> 00:01:51,076
Let's bring Lorestan into view.

12
00:01:56,143 --> 00:02:07,405
Lorestan runs through the western mountains, the Zagros country north of
Khuzestan, with Khorramabad as its largest city, standing in a bend of the
river.

13
00:02:08,067 --> 00:02:19,282
Its name means land of the Lurs, and the Luristan bronzes, decorated weapons
and horse fittings of the Iron Age, come from this region and from
neighbouring Ilam.

14
00:02:19,938 --> 00:02:27,566
Remember the land of the Lurs, where the bronze axes and horse fittings of the
Iron Age were found in the Zagros valleys.

15
00:02:28,261 --> 00:02:31,024
Let's bring Ilam into view.

16
00:02:36,060 --> 00:02:47,148
Ilam is the small south-western province where the Zagros meets the low plains
of Khuzestan, with the city of Ilam in its north, high above the marshes.

17
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:58,760
The territory was known as Poshtkuh-e Lorestan before the name Ilam was
adopted, and the mound of Ali Kosh near Dehloran preserves evidence of
Neolithic settlement.

18
00:02:59,419 --> 00:03:07,012
Remember the little south-western province, where the Neolithic mounds of Ali
Kosh show how early people lived off the land.

19
00:03:07,689 --> 00:03:09,732
Let's bring Isfahan into view.

20
00:03:15,640 --> 00:03:26,728
Isfahan lies in the centre of Iran, the green valley of the Zayanderud river
between the Zagros and the desert, with the city of Isfahan in the middle of
it.

21
00:03:27,393 --> 00:03:42,707
The name appears as Spahan in Sasanian inscriptions describing the province,
the houses of Abyaneh are coated in red earth, and Kashan still grows roses
for its rose water.

22
00:03:43,358 --> 00:03:50,904
Remember the green river valley in the middle of the country, the red-earth
village of Abyaneh and the roses of Kashan.

23
00:03:51,588 --> 00:03:53,840
Let's bring Yazd into view.

24
00:03:58,771 --> 00:04:08,663
Yazd stands in the centre-east of Iran, on the edge of the desert where the
mountains give way to sand, with the city of Yazd at its heart.

25
00:04:09,372 --> 00:04:23,792
Its name is possibly connected with the Sasanian king Yazdegerd I, the
Zoroastrian shrine of Chak Chak near Ardakan attracts a pilgrimage every June,
and Meybod still weaves zilu floor coverings from cotton yarn.

26
00:04:24,455 --> 00:04:32,721
Remember the desert city that kept its old wind towers, the fire temple above
it and the woven floor rugs of Meybod.

27
00:04:33,401 --> 00:04:36,036
Let's bring South Khorasan into view.

28
00:04:40,236 --> 00:04:51,533
South Khorasan lies in the east of Iran, with Birjand as its largest city,
between the old Khorasan in the north and the Baluch lands in the south.

29
00:04:52,198 --> 00:05:05,375
It was created in 2004 from Khorasan, a name referring to the rising sun or
the east, and the Akbariyeh garden at Birjand is one of the nine Persian
gardens inscribed by UNESCO.

30
00:05:06,034 --> 00:05:14,776
Remember the eastern province cut off in 2004, with a UNESCO garden at Birjand
and the saffron fields of Qaen nearby.

31
00:05:15,454 --> 00:05:18,821
Let's bring Chaharmahal and Bakhtiari into view.

32
00:05:23,061 --> 00:05:35,600
Chaharmahal and Bakhtiari is a high province in the centre-west, on the Zagros
plateau above Khuzestan, with Shahrekord as its largest city and Isfahan on
its eastern edge.

33
00:05:36,260 --> 00:05:50,041
Chaharmahal means four regions and Bakhtiari names the tribal community, whose
herders still move their flocks between summer grazing grounds around
Shahrekord and winter quarters down in Khuzestan.

34
00:05:50,694 --> 00:05:59,738
Remember the four regions of the Bakhtiari, whose herders walk their flocks
down to the warm lowlands each winter.

35
00:06:00,439 --> 00:06:02,784
Let's bring Khuzestan into view.

36
00:06:09,365 --> 00:06:19,210
Khuzestan is the south-western province at the head of the Persian Gulf, low
and flat, with Ahvaz as its largest city where the Karun and Dez rivers run.

37
00:06:19,860 --> 00:06:30,100
The name probably means the land of the Khuz, an old name for the people of
Susa, and at Chogha Zanbil stands one of the few ziggurats left outside
Mesopotamia.

38
00:06:30,760 --> 00:06:37,970
Remember the low green province at the head of the Gulf, where the stepped
tower of Chogha Zanbil still stands.

39
00:06:38,797 --> 00:06:42,419
Let's bring Kohgiluyeh and Boyer-Ahmad into view.

40
00:06:54,307 --> 00:07:05,011
Kohgiluyeh and Boyer-Ahmad lies in the south-west, above the Persian Gulf
coast, with Yasuj as its largest city high among the folds of the Zagros.

41
00:07:05,676 --> 00:07:22,344
The name joins the old region of Kohgiluyeh with the Boyer-Ahmadi tribal
group, who speak a Luri language of the south-western Iranian group, and near
Shush two limestone pillars called Dogur Dopa still puzzle visitors.

42
00:07:23,003 --> 00:07:29,563
Remember the two strange pillars above Shush, and the tribe that gives the
province its second name.

43
00:07:30,278 --> 00:07:32,496
Let's bring Fars into view.

44
00:07:39,005 --> 00:07:48,850
Fars lies in the south-central part of Iran, the land the Persians came from,
with Shiraz as its largest city and the Zagros rising along its western edge.

45
00:07:49,503 --> 00:08:08,259
Pars, the old name of this land, gave its name to Persia and to Farsi, and at
Persepolis the ceremonial capital of Darius was burned by Alexander in 330 BC,
while the tomb of Cyrus the Great stands at Pasargadae.

46
00:08:08,924 --> 00:08:17,585
Remember the homeland of the Persians, where Alexander burned Persepolis and
Cyrus is buried at Pasargadae.

47
00:08:18,312 --> 00:08:20,146
Let's bring Kerman into view.

48
00:08:26,353 --> 00:08:37,824
Kerman lies in the south-east of Iran, a dry province of high plains and
desert between Fars in the west and the coast of Hormozgan in the south, with
the city of Kerman in its north.

49
00:08:38,483 --> 00:08:54,503
The mud-brick citadel of Bam was largely destroyed in the earthquake of 2003,
which killed about 26,000 people, and satellites have measured the hottest
ground temperatures on Earth in the Lut desert to the east.

50
00:08:55,153 --> 00:09:05,602
Remember the hot desert province, where the ground has been measured hotter
than anywhere else on Earth and the citadel of Bam fell in 2003.

51
00:09:06,293 --> 00:09:08,383
Let's bring Bushehr into view.

52
00:09:13,210 --> 00:09:23,787
Bushehr runs along the Persian Gulf coast in the south-west, a narrow green
strip between the mountains and the water, with the city of Bushehr on the
shore.

53
00:09:24,444 --> 00:09:40,104
The island port of Siraf was one of the great ports of the Gulf around the
tenth century, trading as far as China, and Iran’s first nuclear power
station, near Bushehr, began supplying power in 2011.

54
00:09:40,763 --> 00:09:46,905
Remember the narrow Gulf coast, where the old island port of Siraf traded with
China.

55
00:09:47,594 --> 00:09:51,390
Let's bring Sistan and Baluchestan into view.

56
00:09:55,309 --> 00:10:06,698
Sistan and Baluchestan is the great south-eastern province, stretching from
the Afghan border to the shore of the Gulf of Oman, with Zahedan as its
largest city in the north.

57
00:10:07,361 --> 00:10:26,477
Sistan comes from Sakastan, the land of the Saka, and Baluchestan is named for
the Baluch people, and at Shahr-i Sokhta archaeologists found a prosthetic eye
in a grave dated to about 2900 to 2800 BC.

58
00:10:27,132 --> 00:10:33,738
Remember the far south-east, where the oldest known artificial eye was dug out
of the mound of Shahr-i Sokhta.

59
00:10:34,541 --> 00:10:36,666
Let's bring Hormozgan into view.

60
00:10:48,714 --> 00:11:00,138
Hormozgan wraps around the Strait of Hormuz in the far south, facing the Gulf
of Oman and the head of the Persian Gulf, with Bandar Abbas as its largest
city on the water.

61
00:11:00,801 --> 00:11:20,421
The province is named for the island and the strait, the Portuguese holding
the island from 1507 until Shah Abbas took it with English help in 1622, and
about a fifth of the world’s oil passes through the strait.

62
00:11:21,079 --> 00:11:29,821
Remember the strait at the mouth of the Gulf, where a fifth of the world’s oil
passes and the Portuguese lost the island to the English.

63
00:11:45,316 --> 00:12:10,876
15 regions of Iran, one for each target of the map: Razavi Khorasan,
Kermanshah, Lorestan, Ilam, Isfahan, Yazd, South Khorasan, Chaharmahal and
Bakhtiari, Khuzestan, Kohgiluyeh and Boyer-Ahmad, Fars, Kerman, Bushehr,
Sistan and Baluchestan and Hormozgan.

64
00:12:11,535 --> 00:12:21,984
Remember the shape of Iran from the inside: the regions nest around each
other, and every name on the map belongs somewhere in this one country.
